title: Eosinophilic Gastroenteritis
---

# Eosinophilic gastroenteritis

- **Definition** - disorder of unknown etiology involving any level of the gastrointestinal tract, characterised by eosinophilic infiltration of the gut wall in the <u>absence of parasitic infection or eosinophilia of other tissues</u>
- **Comorbid conditions:**
  - 50% w/ Hx of other allergic disorders
- **Clinical features of eosinophilic gastroenteritis** - features are related to obstruction and inflammation:
  - <u>Colicky abdominal pain</u> - due to obstruction and inflammation if SB involvement
  - <u>N/V</u> - likely due to obstruction
  - <u>Weight loss</u> - as a result of pain resulting in aversion of food
  - <u>Protein-losing enteropathy</u> - caused by increased gut mucosal permeability resulting in presentation w/ edema and stearrhoea
  - <u>Eosinophilic ascites</u> - due to serosal involvement
- **Ix and Dx** - primarily a Histological Dx:
  - **Bloods** - CBC, IgE:
    - <u>Eos</u> - peripheral Eos usually raised
    - <u>IgE</u> - often raised
  - **Endoscopy** - Bx to demonstrate eosinophilic infiltration into gut wall
- **Mx:**
  - <u>Dietary manipulations</u> - elimination diets (esp. milk); rarely beneficial
  - <u>Pharmacological therapy</u>:
    - Oral prednisolone (20-40 mg/d)
    - Mast cell stabilisers (e.g. sodium gromoglicate)
- **Prognosis** - excellent
